---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="License — fair-code, the Sustainable Use License"
  description="Every impire-io project is source-available under the Sustainable Use License: free to use, modify, and self-host for internal or non-commercial use; offering it to others as a paid product or service requires an agreement."
  project="soulstream"
  active="about"
>
  <header class="hero hero--sub">
    <div class="copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">license · fair-code</p>
      <h1>Free to run. Yours to change. Not yours to sell.</h1>
      <p class="sub">
        Every impire-io project — the five soulsystem components, PRA, and imps — is
        source-available under the <b>Sustainable Use License</b>, the
        <a href="https://faircode.io">fair-code</a> license written by n8n. The code is on
        GitHub, the design logs are public, and self-hosting is free, forever.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="prose">
    <h3>What you can do, no questions asked</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Use it</strong> — run any component for yourself, your team, or your business, on your own machines.</li>
      <li><strong>Modify it</strong> — fork, patch, extend, and rebuild; wire it into your own systems.</li>
      <li><strong>Share it</strong> — pass copies on, free of charge, for non-commercial purposes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What needs an agreement</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Offering the software to others as a paid product or service.</strong> If you
      want to sell it, host it for customers, or build a commercial offering on top of it,
      that requires a separate agreement — get in touch through
      <a href="https://github.com/impire-io">GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's the whole trade: self-hosting stays wide open, and turning the same
      substrate into someone else's paid platform becomes a conversation instead of a
      surprise.
    </p>

    <h3>The MIT history</h3>
    <p>
      Everything published before this change was MIT-licensed, and a granted license is
      irrevocable: <strong>those released versions remain MIT</strong>. The Sustainable Use
      License applies from each component's next release onward. Each repository's
      <code>LICENSE</code> file states this explicitly.
    </p>

    <h3>Why fair-code and not open source</h3>
    <p>
      The Sustainable Use License is not an OSI-approved open-source license, and we don't
      call it one. The ecosystem's bet is that <em>the substrate is the product and you run
      it yourself</em> — that use stays free. What the license closes is the one lane MIT
      left open: a third party packaging the same substrate as a hosted commercial service
      with no relationship to the project. The reasoning, including what would make us
      reverse it, is recorded in the public
      <a href="https://github.com/impire-io/soul-hq">journey log</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>The license text</h3>
    <p>
      The authoritative text lives in each repository's <code>LICENSE</code> file — for
      example
      <a href="https://github.com/impire-io/soulstream/blob/main/LICENSE">soulstream's</a>.
      It is the <a href="https://docs.n8n.io/sustainable-use-license/">Sustainable Use
      License, version 1.0</a>, unchanged.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
